            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Compares two tensors element by element and gives back a tensor of true and false: true wherever a is less than or equal to b.\n\nThe answer is not one yes or no but a whole tensor of them, the same shape as the inputs, holding the outcome at every position. That tensor is a MASK - the way to say \"these elements, not those\" to everything that follows.\n\nWHAT TO DO WITH A MASK:\nMultiply by it and everything that failed becomes zero. t.masked_select takes out just the values where it is true. t.count_nonzero (or t.sum) counts how many passed. To get the PROPORTION that passed, put t.to float32 in front of t.mean: torch will not average a bool tensor (t.mean on it raises 'could not infer output dtype'). Summing and multiplying work on it as it is.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.le\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.le\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor a in:\nThe tensor to test (a list or number is turned into a float32 tensor). Receiving it makes the node compare and send.\n\ntensor b in:\nWhat to test against: another tensor, or a single number used for every element. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it FIRST; until it has received something the node sends nothing. It is converted to tensor a's type, so against an integer tensor a threshold of 4.5 becomes 4.\n\nThe shapes must broadcast: the same shape, a single number, or shapes that differ only where one has length 1 - so a column of thresholds tests each row against its own.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor result:\nA bool tensor, true where a <= b, in the broadcast shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe top strip is tensor a: the whole numbers 0 to 11. The strip under it is the mask - white (1) where the number is at most the slider, black (0) elsewhere. At 5 the cells 0 to 5 are white: the cell holding 5 is included, which is the one difference from t.lt. Drag the slider and the edge moves.\n\nRELATED:\nt.lt leaves equality out. t.ge is the other direction. <= does the same for numbers and NumPy arrays. np.where uses a mask to choose between two arrays.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 855,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Compares two tensors element by element and gives back a tensor of true and false: true wherever a is less than or equal to b.\n\nThe answer is not one yes or no but a whole tensor of them, the same shape as the inputs, holding the outcome at every position. That tensor is a MASK - the way to say \"these elements, not those\" to everything that follows.\n\nWHAT TO DO WITH A MASK:\nMultiply by it and everything that failed becomes zero. t.masked_select takes out just the values where it is true. t.count_nonzero (or t.sum) counts how many passed. To get the PROPORTION that passed, put t.to float32 in front of t.mean: torch will not average a bool tensor (t.mean on it raises 'could not infer output dtype'). Summing and multiplying work on it as it is.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.le\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.le\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor a in:\nThe tensor to test (a list or number is turned into a float32 tensor). Receiving it makes the node compare and send.\n\ntensor b in:\nWhat to test against: another tensor, or a single number used for every element. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it FIRST; until it has received something the node sends nothing. It is converted to tensor a's type, so against an integer tensor a threshold of 4.5 becomes 4.\n\nThe shapes must broadcast: the same shape, a single number, or shapes that differ only where one has length 1 - so a column of thresholds tests each row against its own.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor result:\nA bool tensor, true where a <= b, in the broadcast shape.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe top strip is tensor a: the whole numbers 0 to 11. The strip under it is the mask - white (1) where the number is at most the slider, black (0) elsewhere. At 5 the cells 0 to 5 are white: the cell holding 5 is included, which is the one difference from t.lt. Drag the slider and the edge moves.\n\nRELATED:\nt.lt leaves equality out. t.ge is the other direction. <= does the same for numbers and NumPy arrays. np.where uses a mask to choose between two arrays."
        },
